1. e4 d6 (1... g6 { this is usually called The Modern, I think of it as a close
cousin to the Pirc, black delays developing their g8 knight. } 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 d6 (3... a6 4. Be3 b5 5. Qd2 Bb7 6. f3 d6 { transposes to 3...d6 }) 4. Be3 a6 5. Qd2 b5 { Not a bad option for those determined to get an aggressive and violent
position as black. While the computer despises this position for black it scores
quite well in human play. Let's talk about black's dreams: by delaying Nf6 black
prevents any Bh6 plan and leaves e4-e5 without a point. The drawbacks of this
plan are obvious: The king on e8 will be scrambling in the pocket, it's hard for
Rh8 to get involved and the g8 might find it impossible to actually ever get
out. } 6. O-O-O Bb7 7. f3 { SPARRING POSITI $146 } 7... Nd7 8. h4 { our play could not
be more direct } 8... h5 (8... h6 9. Nh3 Rc8 10. Kb1 c5 11. g4 { we work to make
the development of black's knight difficult } 11... Ngf6 (11... b4 { this move
comes with drawbacks as our Bf1 now sniffs an attacking post on c4 } 12. Ne2 Qc7 13. g5 $1 { Black either
invites our knight to g5 by taking or never develops their g8 knight } { [%c_effect g5;square;g5;type;GreatFind;persistent;true] }) 12. g5 hxg5 13. Nxg5 { this knight is a real terror for black's king }) (8... Ngf6 9. g4 { since h5 is a mortal threat black can't develop peaceably } 9... h6 10. Nh3 { with g5 next }) (8... c5 $2 { It's important to understand that our h5 is a mortal threat: it will develop our
rook and hang a sword over black's kingside. } { [%c_effect c5;square;c5;type;Mistake;persistent;true] } 9. h5 cxd4 10. Bxd4 Bxd4 (10... Ngf6 11. hxg6 fxg6 12. Nh3 { The knight on g5 will be a terrorist. }) 11. Qxd4 Ngf6 12. hxg6 fxg6 13. Nh3 { The knight on g5 will be a terrorist. }) 9. Nh3 { Now we know where our knight wants to go. Black can now play various moves but
we are guaranteed a sustained attack with our $146 on g5. } 9... Rc8 (9... Ngf6 10. Ng5 O-O 11. g4 $1 { [%c_effect g4;square;g4;type;GreatFind;persistent;true] } 11... hxg4 12. h5 $1 { [%c_effect h5;square;h5;type;GreatFind;persistent;true] } 12... gxh5 13. fxg4 $18 { A classic breakthrough, our queen is coming to h2 and
black is going to get mated. }) (9... c5 10. dxc5 Nxc5 11. Ng5 Rc8 12. Bd4 Nf6 13. Nd5 { Let's talk about this move: our intention is not to double black's
pawns but rather to prevent Qa5 and remove a key defender from black's king. } 13... O-O 14. g4 $1 { We've
seen this before. } { [%c_effect g4;square;g4;type;GreatFind;persistent;true] } 14... hxg4 15. h5 gxh5 16. fxg4) 10. Ng5 c5 11. e5 $1 { This is the kind of
violence black deserves. Maybe they'll develop their pieces in the next game. } { [%c_effect e5;square;e5;type;GreatFind;persistent;true] } 11... cxd4 (11... dxe5 12. dxe5 Bxe5 (12... Nxe5 $2 { allowing our R on d1 and the knight
we have worked so hard for on g5 to fulfill their ambitions in life } { [%c_effect e5;square;e5;type;Mistake;persistent;true] } 13. Qxd8+ Rxd8 14. Rxd8+ Kxd8 15. f4 { There is no way to stop the rampaging knight. } 15... Ng4 16. Nxf7+ Ke8 17. Nxh8 Nxe3 18. Nxg6 $18 { we have more toys }) 13. Nxf7 Kxf7 14. Qxd7 Qxd7 15. Rxd7 $16 { rook on the seventh and several weaknesses to look
at }) 12. e6 $1 { the
diabolical point } { [%c_effect e6;square;e6;type;GreatFind;persistent;true] } 12... fxe6 (12... dxe3 13. exf7+ Kf8 14. Ne6+ Kxf7 15. Nxd8+ $18 { queen gone }) 13. Bxd4 e5 14. Bd3 Nf8 15. Bf2 { Black will be playing
without their kingside pieces and the poor king will never be safe. }) 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 g6 (3... c6 { The Prybyl } 4. f4 { To play this move we need to
understand that the normal g6 leads to an Austrian attack where c6 doesn't
really make sense. } 4... Qa5 { This is obviously kind of a strange move, but it's
the most common approach by black here. } (4... g6 5. Nf3 Bg7 6. Bd3 O-O 7. O-O b5 8. e5 { Black's pieces don't make sense. Note that normally black would play
not c6 but c5 to challenge our center. }) 5. Bd3 e5 6. Nf3 { White is much
better: we have the center, development and a setup that should strike us as
familiar. }) 4. Be3 { White has a simple plan: 0-0-0, Qd2-Bh6, h4-h5, wiggle the
lady into h6 and then mate black's king. Let's contrast this with the Austrian
attack (6.f4). That's a plan that plays for space, we play for mate. } 4... Bg7 (4... Ng4 { The problem with this move is that the knight won't be able to stay
here, so we'll likely see a retreat with loss of tempo. } 5. Bg5 h6 6. Bh4 Bg7 7. Qd2 g5 (7... O-O 8. O-O-O c6 9. f4 $1 { we are now going to roll black up } { [%c_effect f4;square;f4;type;GreatFind;persistent;true] } 9... b5 10. e5 $1 { Ng4 is
gone, h3 on its way } { [%c_effect e5;square;e5;type;GreatFind;persistent;true] }) 8. Bg3 { black will pay for g5 with a later h4 } 8... e5 9. d5 f5 { Every Russian schoolboy knows that they should take back with the
g-pawn. But unfortunately for black the g-pawn is on g5 instead of g6. Now the
light squares are critically weak. } 10. exf5 Bxf5 11. O-O-O Nd7 12. h4 { All of
our pieces will be working while black's king has yet to find a safe space. }) (4... a6 5. Qd2 { The following sequence is like a machine: Qd2, 0-0-0, f3, h4.
And we see it in most of our lines. We really only have to think when black
responds to the mortal threat of h5. But we know what we should be thinking: how
can we involve our knight on g1 $1 } 5... b5 6. f3 Nbd7 7. O-O-O Bb7 8. h4 h5 (8... c5 9. g4 { With an attack very similar to our Modern lines. }) 9. Nh3 Bg7 10. Ng5 { We have transposed to the modern lines with g6 and a6. }) (4... c6 { this is
a fancy move that I have a lot of sympathy for. Black says: if you are going to
put a bishop on h6 I don't see why I have to move mine to g7. } 5. Qd2 b5 6. f3 Nbd7 7. g4 { our play is simple and direct } 7... Nb6 (7... Bg7 8. h4 { Again: h5
is a mortal threat. } 8... h5 9. g5 Nh7 10. f4 Nb6 11. b3 { With a fantastic
game, note that if black wants to play with his $146 on h7 they'll have to weaken
their king. }) 8. h4 h5 9. g5 Nfd7 10. d5 $1 { A very concrete move that needs to
be remembered and studied. We break down the c6-b5 pawn chain and aim to use the
d4 square as a trampoline. } { [%c_effect d5;square;d5;type;GreatFind;persistent;true] } 10... Ne5 (10... cxd5 11. Bd4 $1 { [%c_effect d4;square;d4;type;GreatFind;persistent;true] } 11... e5 12. Bxb6 Nxb6 13. Bxb5+ $18 { white wins a pawn and the attack continues }) 11. Bd4 $1 { [%c_effect d4;square;d4;type;GreatFind;persistent;true] } 11... b4 12. dxc6 $1 { [%c_effect c6;square;c6;type;GreatFind;persistent;true] } 12... bxc3 13. Qxc3 { f4 comes
next with a great game }) 5. Qd2 c6 (5... O-O 6. O-O-O c6 7. Bh6 { A great rule of
thumb in fianchetto positions: only spend a tempo on Bh6 if you are playing for
mate. We are playing for mate. } 7... b5 8. f3 Qa5 9. h4 { The plan seen here
shows the general mechanics of our mating attack: Bh6, h4-h5, bring the lady to
h6. } 9... b4 10. Nb1 Qxa2 { Black may feel like they've accomplished something,
but the queen is a long way from her man and we have the jump. } 11. h5 Nbd7 12. hxg6 fxg6 { only move } 13. Bxg7 Kxg7 14. Qh6+ { The lady has arrived. } 14... Kf7 15. Nh3 Ke8 16. Ng5 { Black is going to get mated. }) 6. Bh6 Bxh6 7. Qxh6 { SPARRING POSITI $146 } 7... Qa5 (7... b5 8. Bd3 Nbd7 9. Nf3 e5 10. h3 $16 { Black's
king and coordination are permanently damaged. Now we can calmly castle short. }) 8. Bd3 c5 (8... Qh5 9. Qd2 O-O 10. h4 $1 { your queen will move and I will get
in h5 $1- > } { [%c_effect h4;square;h4;type;GreatFind;persistent;true] }) (8... Nbd7 9. Nf3 Qh5 10. Qd2 O-O 11. h4 $1 { your queen will move and I will get
